---
/**
 * K Culture Wire — **카탈로그가 커지면 한 편이 더 멀리 가나.** (`/catalogue-reach`)
 *
 * ── ⛔ 이 지면이 지키는 것 ────────────────────────────────────
 * ⛔ **줄세우지 않는다.** 「어느 회사가 제일 멀리 가나」를 안 낸다. 크기 띠로 나란히 놓는다.
 * ⛔ **가운데값과 평균을 같이** 낸다. 둘이 어긋나는 것 자체가 이 표에서 제일 말이 많은 자리다.
 * ⛔ **한 편짜리 회사를 띠에 안 섞는다.** 1 → 2 편의 뜀은 고른 탓일 수 있어 따로 두고 그렇게 적는다.
 * ⛔ 「멀리 갔다 = 인기」로 안 쓴다. 우리가 본 것은 **차트에 걸린 나라 수**뿐이다.
 * ⛔ 광고 자리를 만들지 않았다. Riot Production(App 866800) 승인 전이다.
 * ⚠ 수를 손으로 적지 않는다. 전부 `wikitip-catalogue-reach.json` 에서 읽는다.
 */
import WikiTip from '../../layouts/WikiTip.astro';
import KcwRelatedArticles from '../../components/KcwRelatedArticles.astro';
import data from '../../data/wikitip-catalogue-reach.json';

const 첫띠 = data.bands[0];
const 끝띠 = data.bands[data.bands.length - 1];
const 한편 = data.singleTitleFirms;

const TITLE = 'Does a bigger catalogue travel further per title?';
const DESC =
  `Across ${data.firmsWithCharting} companies with at least one charting Korean title, catalogue size `
  + `correlates with total reach (r = ${data.rTotal}) but not with reach per title (r = ${data.rPerTitle}). `
  + `A company with ${끝띠.label.toLowerCase()} gets ${끝띠.perTitleMedian} markets out of the median title; `
  + `one with ${첫띠.titlesMedian} gets ${첫띠.perTitleMedian}.`;

/** 막대 — 가장 큰 값을 100 으로. 띠마다가 아니라 **표 전체**를 한 자로 재야 평평한 것이 보인다 */
const 최대편당 = Math.max(...data.bands.map((b) => b.perTitleMedian));
const 최대총 = Math.max(...data.bands.map((b) => b.totalMarketsMedian));

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Dataset',
  name: TITLE,
  description: DESC,
  creator: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'K Culture Wire' },
  isBasedOn: ['https://www.netflix.com/tudum/top10', 'https://www.wikidata.org'],
};
---

<WikiTip title={TITLE} description={DESC} jsonLd={jsonLd}
  canonical="https://www.kculturewire.com/catalogue-reach">
  <h1>{TITLE}</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    A company that has put twenty titles into a Netflix chart is in more countries than one that has
    put in two. That much is arithmetic. The question worth asking is whether each individual title
    goes further — whether scale buys distance, or only sums.
  </p>
  <p class="q">{data.question}</p>

  <section>
    <h2>Two numbers that behave differently</h2>
    <p class="note lead-in"><b>How this is counted.</b> {data.unit}</p>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Catalogue size against…</th><th>Correlation</th><th>Reading</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td class="nm">Total markets the catalogue reached</td>
            <td class="tot">{data.rTotal}</td>
            <td>Rises with size, as it must — more titles, more countries between them</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="edge">
            <td class="nm">Markets per title</td>
            <td class="tot">{data.rPerTitle}</td>
            <td>Flat. Nothing there, and if anything it leans the other way</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      Size is taken as its logarithm, because the step from two titles to four is a bigger change in a
      company than the step from twenty to twenty-two. Both figures cover the
      {' '}{data.multiTitleFirms} companies with two or more charting titles. Reach is out of
      {' '}{data.marketCount} markets, and {data.firmsWithCharting} of the {data.firmsTotal} companies
      we hold have at least one title that charted somewhere — the remaining one has none, so it
      cannot appear here at all.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The same thing as a table</h2>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Charting titles</th><th>Companies</th><th>Markets per title (median)</th>
            <th>Markets per title (mean)</th><th>Total markets reached (median)</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {data.bands.map((b) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="nm">{b.label}</td>
              <td>{b.firms}</td>
              <td class="tot">{b.perTitleMedian}</td>
              <td>{b.perTitleMean}</td>
              <td class="tot">{b.totalMarketsMedian}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="twin">
      <div>
        <div class="shape-nm">Markets per title <span class="fine">(median)</span></div>
        {data.bands.map((b) => (
          <div class="row">
            <span class="rl">{b.label}</span>
            <span class="rb"><i style={`width:${Math.round((100 * b.perTitleMedian) / 최대편당)}%`}></i></span>
            <span class="rv">{b.perTitleMedian}</span>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="shape-nm">Total markets reached <span class="fine">(median)</span></div>
        {data.bands.map((b) => (
          <div class="row">
            <span class="rl">{b.label}</span>
            <span class="rb"><i style={`width:${Math.round((100 * b.totalMarketsMedian) / 최대총)}%`}></i></span>
            <span class="rv">{b.totalMarketsMedian}</span>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      Both columns are drawn against the same scale as each other's own largest bar. The left one is
      flat from {첫띠.label.toLowerCase()} to {끝띠.label.toLowerCase()}; the right one climbs from
      {' '}{첫띠.totalMarketsMedian} to {끝띠.totalMarketsMedian} markets.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Where the median and the mean disagree</h2>
    <p class="warn">
      Read the two per-title columns against each other. The median barely moves, but the mean falls
      from {첫띠.perTitleMean} to {끝띠.perTitleMean}. That is not a contradiction — it says the small
      companies in this table include a few whose one or two titles travelled unusually far, and those
      outliers pull a small group's mean up much harder than a large group's.
      {' '}<b>A big catalogue is not a catalogue of big titles. It is a catalogue with fewer surprises
      in it.</b>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Companies with exactly one charting title are counted apart</h2>
    <p class="warn">
      There are {한편.firms} of them — more than half of everyone here — and their median title reached
      {' '}{한편.perTitleMedian} markets, far below every band above. {한편.reachedOneMarketOnly} of them
      reached exactly one market, and one of them reached {한편.biggest}.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      <b>⚠ We do not read that gap as the value of a second title.</b> A company only appears in this
      table once something of its own has charted, so a company with two charting titles has already
      shown something the one-title companies have not. The step from one to two is entangled with
      which companies get to take it, and this data cannot separate the two. The flat line above
      starts at two titles for that reason.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The same picture by our own coverage grade</h2>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Grade</th><th>Companies</th><th>Charting titles (median)</th>
            <th>Total markets (median)</th><th>Markets per title (median)</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {data.grades.map((g) => (
            <tr class={g.grade === 'A' ? 'edge' : ''}>
              <td class="nm">{g.grade}</td>
              <td>{g.firms}</td>
              <td>{g.titlesMedian}</td>
              <td class="tot">{g.marketsMedian}</td>
              <td class="tot">{g.perTitleMedian}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      Grade A companies hold {data.grades[0].titlesMedian} charting titles at the median against
      {' '}{data.grades[1].titlesMedian} for grade B — four times as many — and reach
      {' '}{data.grades[0].marketsMedian} markets against {data.grades[1].marketsMedian}. Per title,
      they are at {data.grades[0].perTitleMedian} and {data.grades[1].perTitleMedian}: the same number.
      The grades describe how much we can see of a company, not how well it travels.
    </p>
  </section>

  <KcwRelatedArticles page="/catalogue-reach" />

  <section class="src">
    <h2>Where these numbers come from, and what they cannot answer</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Netflix and Wikidata</b> — {data.source}</li>
      <li><b>Reach counts countries, not viewers.</b> {data.cannotAnswer}</li>
      <li>
        <b>A credit is not a share of the work.</b> Wikidata records that a company is attached to a
        title, not how much of it the company held, and titles credited to several companies are
        counted for each of them.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Only {data.firmCoveragePc}% of titles carry a company at all</b> — {data.titlesWithFirm} of
        {' '}{data.titlesAsked}. Everything here is measured on that share, and a company whose titles
        sit in the missing {(100 - data.firmCoveragePc).toFixed(1)}% is absent rather than small.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Corporate groups are not merged.</b> Companies inside one group are listed separately
        because we did not confirm the ownership tree, and guessing it would change the answer.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">
      <a href="/who-makes-it">How few companies fill half the shelf</a> ·{' '}
      <a href="/rank-shape">Where on the chart Korean titles sit</a> ·{' '}
      <a href="/about">How we count</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</WikiTip>

<style>
  .twin { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 1.4rem; margin: 1.2rem 0; }
  @media (max-width: 640px) { .twin { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }
  .shape-nm { font-weight: 600; margin-bottom: 0.45rem; }
  .row { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.5rem; margin: 0.25rem 0; font-size: 0.85rem; }
  .rl { flex: 0 0 6.5rem; opacity: 0.75; }
  .rb { flex: 1; height: 0.7rem; background: currentColor; opacity: 0.12; border-radius: 2px; }
  .rb i { display: block; height: 100%; background: currentColor; opacity: 0.55; border-radius: 2px; }
  .rv { flex: 0 0 2.4rem; text-align: right; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
</style>
